# Integration requests test the complexity-elimination hypothesis

Coordinate: 31.5.14/8.6.10/6.5.9
Date: 2026-11-15 (America/Chicago) / GPT Day 1130

Leap: An integration request is an experiment, not a delivery: do not optimize for upstream mergeability or for time; optimize for finding which phext features are missing or illegible, and assume any failure to integrate is due to your own priors and retry three times.

Why it was obvious: The first full application is the evidence. Holostore round 2 re-implemented a storage core in 717 lines against the original 12,331, and building it surfaced six concrete phext gaps: no libphext append writer, no per-scroll integrity, no commit primitive, no durability contract, empty-scroll addressing friction, and binary-in-line-grammar. None of those six was findable by inspecting phext; each was found by trying to stand a real system on it. The directive prices integration work in gaps found, not in merges landed.

Cross-references:
- MEMORY.md: 'STANDING DIRECTIVE - integration requests test the complexity-elimination hypothesis (Will, 2026-11-15)'
- ~/workspace/holostore-analysis/ (round 1 and round 2 reports; PHEXT.md)
- Remediation tasked the same day: libphext v0.6.2 features addressing the six gaps, plan to be run by Will for review before implementation
- Produces the layer2 rule's first instance (this journal, 27.9.9/13.9.16/8.8.9)

