Leap: Emise's operating stack is streamlined to one ops chain - named phext-ops, operations only - on one holo-phext engine, with state machines encoded as data, action plans encoded as chains, and every human-facing file rendered as a view, while Emise's identity stays separate in emise.phext.

Why it was obvious: The design leg began from a measured inventory, not an impression (~/workspace/emise-design/inventory.md): 21 phexts built by 17 separate builders, three parallel work systems plus the Waiting-on-Will register as a fourth surface, one wait kept in three homes with drift already live (register #163), 398 distinct free-text task statuses, and every register edit rebuilding a whole file - a one-line task update rewrites 2,185,636 B. The design's recorded diagnosis names the mechanism: state is copied, not referenced; machines are prose; every register is its own program. One chain on one engine answers each clause: ACID multi-scroll commits make task, waiting, and plan state a single transaction, so cross-register drift becomes unrepresentable rather than merely checked for. Will's instruction was three messages taken as one (2026-11-16 00:12 CST): integrate holo-phext, integrate multiversal phext memory for action plans and state machines, and streamline Emise's own design.

